+## Adapter-model accounting (R13): what changed outside the agy adapter
+
+**Short answer.** The adapter model held for agy, but only because a large neutral core was built first. On the corrected diff, the run changed 104 `src/` files outside `src/harness/agy/` (+7673/−3137). The adapter's own source is 11 files (+3799). After the neutral core landed, the agy leaf beads made three small production edits outside the adapter that the R13 target did not allow (+19/−4 lines). Then main's merge review (S1) reopened the run: making agy see informational notices between check-ins took a dedicated core bead, ht-poh.53 (`414c918b`), of about +290 lines in 6 core files, plus ht-poh.57's wake-gate fix (`8aa6c95c`, +~30 in 2 of those files). So the target was missed: narrowly at the leaves, and with one real core gap that HookPull had left (notices rode only on lifecycle check-ins, which Claude's push channel never needed). Main's second review then found that the HookPull offer ledger evicted still-pending offers at its 256-id cap, breaking the 3-offer / 60 s bounds on an ordinary backlog of truncated messages; fixing it (ht-poh.59, .60) cost another ~+270/−140 in `src/delivery/ledger.rs`, `src/service/delivery_pull.rs` and `src/ports.rs`. All of this is neutral HookPull core, but agy is its only user, so it is agy-driven. The bigger costs, each recorded as an R13 finding, were the neutral seams agy needed: `hook_step`, `local_evidence`, bootstrap V2 and HookPull.
